 05_5	Here are all ten estimates. Some are too low and some too high. What do the ten average?
 05_6	Exactly five, the true count. The largest number on its own averages only four.
 05_7	A recipe is unbiased if its estimates average to the true count, whatever that count is. A single small company isn't a proof. So why does adding the average gap work?
+06_1	Here's why, starting with the ten pairs from five boxes. Each pair leaves three boxes unseen. Its two numbers cut them into three gaps, below the smaller, between the two, and above the larger.
+06_2	One and two leave gaps of zero, zero and three. Two and five leave one, two and zero. Every pair has its own three gaps, and they always add up to three.
+06_3	A list can also be read backwards. One, two and zero says skip one box, take the next, skip two, take the next, and stop. That rebuilds two and five.
+06_4	So pairs and gap lists match up exactly. The ten lists are every way to split three unseen boxes into three gaps, each exactly once. And since each pair is one in ten, so is each list.
+06_5	Now let the gap below and the gap above trade places. For one and two, zero, zero and three becomes three, zero and zero. That's the list for four and five, another row of the table, just as likely.
+06_6	Every row has a partner like that, and a row with equal gaps below and above is its own partner. So the below column and the above column hold the same ten numbers, in a different order.
+06_7	Trading the gap between with the gap above works the same way. So all three columns hold the same ten numbers, and they must have the same average.
+06_8	Add up each column. Each holds ten boxes, over ten pairs, so each gap averages one box. Three unseen boxes, shared evenly by three gaps. That includes the top gap, above the larger number.
+06_9	Now back to the friends' five boxes. Five numbers cut the unseen boxes into six gaps, but only five arcs are drawn. Where is the sixth gap?
+06_10	Above sixty. It's the top gap, which nobody can see. In the small company, the top gap averaged one box, just like the gaps below it.
+07_1	Now the same argument for any count. Call the count N. The friends' five numbers cut the N minus five unseen boxes into six gaps, five they can see and the top gap they can't.
+07_2	The matching from the table still works. Every handful of five gives a list of six gaps adding up to N minus five, and every such list builds back its own handful.
+07_3	Every handful is equally likely, so every list is too. Letting gaps trade places gives another list, just as likely, so all six gaps have the same average. Together they always hold N minus five boxes, so each gap averages N minus five, over six.
+07_4	That includes the top gap, which the recipe can't see. So the recipe guesses it with the average of the five gaps it can see. For the friends, that's eleven.
+07_5	But eleven is one handful's average, and another handful would give another number. In the small company, each pair guesses its top gap with the average of its two gaps below. Row by row, the guess and the top gap agree only three times in ten.
+07_6	Yet over all ten pairs, the guesses average one box, and so do the top gaps. For the friends it's the same. Over all handfuls, each seen gap averages N minus five, over six, so the average of the five does too, exactly like the top gap.
+07_7	So for every handful, the count is the largest number plus the top gap, and the estimate is the largest number plus the guess. Over all handfuls, the guess and the top gap have the same average, so the estimates average the count. The recipe is unbiased, whatever the count.
+07_8	What does the recipe do with a handful of just one box?
+07_9	It doubles the number and subtracts one. One box leaves two gaps, and the gap below is the only clue to the gap above. Box thirty-seven has thirty-six boxes below it, so the recipe says seventy-three.
+07_10	Nothing in the proof needed five. With any handful size, the numbers cut the unseen boxes into one more gap than there are numbers, all with the same average. So the recipe always adds the largest minus the size, over the size, and it's right on average for every count, as long as the handful is fair.
